From therneau at mayo.edu  Wed Jan  5 13:54:05 2005
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Wed, 05 Jan 2005 07:54:05 -0600
Subject: Re: Conditional logistic regression in S vs. SAS...
Message-ID: <200501051354.j05Ds5g24938@respect.mayo.edu>

Katherine Hoggatt asked about the ties option in  SAS vs Splus, and why they
look different:

   If data is discrete, i.e., the time scale has only a few distinct values,
then the appropriate option for ties is the exact partial likelihood.  SAS
calls this the "discrete" option and Splus the "exact" option.  SAS phreg
also allows the exact marginal likelihood, for which they use the label
"exact", Splus coxph doesn't compute that particular choice for ties.

   If I were doing it over again, I'd have used the label "discrete" as
well, just because it seems to be easier for users to remember "discrete
option for discrete data".  It seemed obvious at the time to label the method
with its formal name, but then not everyone is as conversant as I with the 
fine details of coxph calculations :-).

Notes:
  1. The exact partial likelihood is also the correct choice for matched
case-control data: set time=1 for all the data (or any other positive number
of your choice), status = 1 for case, 0 for control, and fit a stratified
Cox model where each case/control group is a separate strata.  If you set
up the data in this particular way, the exact partial likelihood turns out to
be identical to the logistic model likelihood for matched case control data.
(I've never quite decided if this is just an algebraic accident, or something
deep and significant).  The SAS phreg documentation has one set up the dummy
time variable in a slightly more complicated way; that works too but is more
bother.
   However, note that if you have the situation of 1 case matched
to 1 or more controls, then all of the options for ties in coxph give exactly
the same answer!  The option only matters witn m:n matching when m>1 (multiple
cases per case/control group), a type of study that I have never encountered in
all my years in medical research.  A lot of worry is expended needlessly.

  2. In the limited number of cases that I've looked at, the exact marginal
likelihood results were very close to those for the Efron approximation.
Consequently, I don't expect our group to every bother adding it to coxph.
(It involves numerical evaluation of an integral, so is a major nuisance).

  3. In my definition of discrete data, by "only a few" unique time points
I mean very few, like 5 to 10.  Beyond that I find the Efron approx to be
just fine.  As a consequence, I almost never use the exact partial likelihood.
There is a very clever computational approach due to Gail et al for the
exact partial likelihood, implimented in SAS, and again our group just hasn't
found it a compelling priority for addition to coxph.  
   So if you do have a large discrete data set, SAS will be much faster.
On the other hand for (start, stop] data, which we see every day, the code
for coxph contains computational tricks that phreg hasn't implimented, and
Splus will be much faster.  (In both cases we are talking factors of 5-10
for a data set with 10000 observations).
 

(Some of this discussion can be found in the book by Therneau and Grambsch).

        Terry Therneau
        Biostatistics, Mayo Clinic

