I am trying to create a bubble plot using S-Plus in Windows.
I have drawn a plot using the graphs menus, in which my z variable (ie. the 
variable which determines the size of the symbols) is a frequency variable.  
However, the size of the symbols is not proportional to the frequency (the 
symbol used for a frequency of 10 is not double the size of the symbol used 
for a frequency of 5 etc).  In the "Vary Symbols" menu, there is an option 
to change the maximum and minimum size of the symbols but not how they vary 
for different values of z.

Does anyone have experience with this kind of problem? Is there a solution 
(either using the menus or the command line -I have tried playing around 
with the "symbols" command but to no avail)?

Any help will be gratefully recieved.

Fay Cafferty

From rmh at temple.edu  Fri Jan  7 16:43:17 2005
From: rmh at temple.edu (Richard M. Heiberger)
Date: Fri, 07 Jan 2005 11:43:17 -0500
Subject: Re: Bubble plots
Message-ID: <e9631cdf.bb017817.81d4a00@po-d.temple.edu>

Example from ?GuiPlot

> x <- 1:30 
> mydata <- data.frame(x=x, cosx=cos(x), sinx=sin(x)) 
> guiPlot("Bubble", DataSetValues=mydata) 
[1] "GS1"

Same data in symbols(), unfortunrately it is stretched as the circles are
interpreted in data units on both axes, not in plotting inches.
> symbols(y=mydata$cos, x=mydata$x, circles=abs(mydata$sinx))
> symbols(y=mydata$cos, x=mydata$x, circles=abs(mydata$sinx)/10, inches=FALSE)

The ?symbols command says that the length for circles is the radius.
So take the square root and then the area will be proportional to the number
you are using:

> mydata <- data.frame(x=x, cosx=cos(x), sinx=sqrt(sin(x)) )
> guiPlot("Bubble", DataSetValues=mydata) 
[1] "GS2"
> 

