From rmh at temple.edu  Thu Jan 13 04:01:15 2005
From: rmh at temple.edu (Richard M. Heiberger)
Date: Wed, 12 Jan 2005 23:01:15 -0500
Subject: Re: Identifyiing points in a qqplot
Message-ID: <f670947a.bdd2b302.81ff400@po-d.temple.edu>

## Original question 
j <- length(Wolf01102005$WLFSEC)
qqplot(Wolf01102005$WLFSEC, rnorm(j))
identify(Wolf01102005$WLFSEC, rnorm(j))

## I reformatted the question with proper spacing to improve legibility
## 1. space on both sides of arrow " <- "
## 2. space after commas

## rnorm() generates random numbers.  The first call to rnorm()
## generated one set of random numbers.  The second call to rnorm()
## generated a different set of random numbers, which is not what you
## want.

## Since you are comparing to the normal distribution, it would be
## better to let S construct the comparison values by using qqnorm().
## Save the values it creates and use them for the identify().

Wolf <- rt(100, df=6)
Wolf.xy <- qqnorm(Wolf)
identify(Wolf.xy)

## If you want to compare your observed data to another empirical
## distribution and then use identify, you should also save the result of
## the call to qqplot rather than use the input data.  The reason for
## this recommendation is that qqplot permits x and y to have different
## lengths and qqplot sorts both vectors before plotting them.  qqplot
## shows different behavior than qqnorm.  qqnorm retains the original
## order of the input vector.

x <- rnorm(10)
names(x) <- letters[1:10]
y <- rt(15, df=5)
qq.xy <- qqplot(x, y)
x
y
qq.xy
id.xy <- identify(qq.xy)
id.xy
names(qq.xy$x)[id.xy]

